#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161745 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161930 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1cc5827c-739e-52fd-9f10-a75bf2c18c22" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a \"Less Than\" Probability With the Exponential Distribution"}] :question/id #uuid "6d3ad56f-a242-5335-aa48-e2608b1b973d" :question/math-academy-id "e-16584" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X\\sim \\textrm{Exp}\\left(1\\right),$ calculate $P\\left(X < 2\\right).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places." :question/worked-solution "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/f3/f3440005b2b4e7ce1cc2eed5825d11cb309522a6807bcd18ffada189fadd0cda.svg)\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp} \\left( 1 \\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/1f/1f2f28b0f36aeb85a07076e1507c9561b707bf7d00de727998b02075b5458da4.svg)\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P\\left(X < 2\\right)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<2) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{{e}^{-x}|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.8647\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161746 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161931 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1677dd6-74e4-5778-bbba-fbb230c7646a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a \"Greater Than\" Probability With the Exponential Distribution"}] :question/id #uuid "f1adfc5c-78aa-5f14-bec5-cf763d86e5af" :question/math-academy-id "e-16585" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X\\sim \\textrm{Exp}\\left(\\dfrac 23\\right),$ calculate $P\\left(X \\geq 3\\right).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places." :question/worked-solution "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/49/49fa146efc8029727469bc5909c2a946c9cb96383976d4096d455b423a428479.svg)\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp} \\left( \\dfrac 23 \\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/72/721ca1810b816431f99bb8294f4ad12ff125342640a925035487c8ecace4eff6.svg)Therefore, we can calculate $P\\left(X \\geq 3\\right)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3) & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-2x/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{{e}^{-2x/3}|}_{3}^{\\infty } \\\\  & ={e}^{-2} \\\\  & ≈0.1353\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161747 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161932 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c89beba-5605-514e-82c3-8e8145b178c9" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability on a Bounded Interval Using the Exponential Distribution"}] :question/id #uuid "fffe6dba-c0a1-5644-a975-6d51b6de2f98" :question/math-academy-id "e-16586" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X\\sim \\textrm{Exp}\\left(2\\right),$ calculate $P\\left(\\dfrac 12 < X < 2\\right).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places." :question/worked-solution "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/e7/e77daa45c1026175eec99e71cb336ad5b8c2a46683170b0f9357a4ad7e9d1bf5.svg)\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp} \\left( 2 \\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/e0/e0b6d1388350fabce97f26601d512e60cfe6ac3ab41230671718ca034c5c3860.svg)Therefore, we can calculate $P\\left(\\dfrac 12 < X < 2\\right)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{1}{2}<X<2) & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}2{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{{e}^{-2x}|}_{1/2}^{2} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-4}+{e}^{-1} \\\\  & ≈0.3496\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161748 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161933 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aab06631-cd2c-57d2-9518-25f7a31c4b6b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Probabilities Using the Memoryless Property"}] :question/id #uuid "88232e58-999e-5ac8-8f09-8a4b204ec51c" :question/math-academy-id "e-16587" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X\\sim \\textrm{Exp}\\left(4\\right),$ calculate $P\\left(X \\leq 7 \\, \\bigg |\\, X > 5\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a random variable $X \\sim \\mathrm{Exp}(\\lambda)$ and two positive real numbers $s, t,$ the memoryless property of the exponential distribution states that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t \\, \\big |\\, X>s) = P(X > t) = e^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the complementary conditional probability as follows:\n\n$$\nP(X\\leq s+t \\, \\big|\\, X>s) = 1-P(X>s+t \\, \\big |\\, X>s) = 1-e^{-\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda = 4,$ $s=5,$ and $s+t=7.$ From this, we can find $t$ as follows:\n\n$$\nt = (t+s) - s =7 - 5 = 2\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 7\\,|\\,X>5) & =1-{e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-(4)\\cdot (2)} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-8} \\\\  & ≈0.999\\,664.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
